{"name":"Aqua Velvet","hex":"#00a29e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aqua","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water (as a compound).","A shade of colour, usually a mix of blue and green, most often similar to the colour cyan, though also sometimes closer to turquoise or aquamarine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aqua"},{"term":"velvet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closely woven fabric (originally of silk, now also of cotton or man-made fibres) with a thick short pile on one side.","Very fine fur, including the skin and fur on a deer's antlers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with velvet or with a covering of a similar texture.","To coat raw meat in starch, then in oil, preparatory to frying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velvet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velvet","blocks":[{"text":"Velvet is a type of woven fabric with a dense, even pile that gives it a distinctive soft feel. Historically, velvet was typically made from silk. Modern velvet can be made from silk, linen, cotton, wool, synthetic fibers, silk-cotton blends, or synthetic-natural fiber blends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet","wikidata":"Q243519"},{"title":"Aqua (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Aqua is a variation of the color cyan. The normalized color coordinates for the two web colors named aqua and cyan are identical. It was one of the three secondary colors of the RGB color model used on computer and television displays. In the HSV color wheel aqua is precisely halfway between blue and green. However, aqua is not the same as the primary subtractive color named process cyan used in printing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.